ZTEXT00100000d1297a95bcf0000000
ZERO RGB PICTURE FORMAT — ZPIX0001

This original format stores lossless 24-bit RGB images as repeated-pixel runs.
Its extension is .zpix. Pixels are ordered left to right, then top to bottom.
A run may cross a row boundary. There is no alpha channel, palette, orientation
metadata, color profile, audio track or video timeline in this version.

All binary integers below are unsigned little-endian. The header is 64 bytes.

Offset  Size  Meaning
0       8     ASCII bytes ZPIX0001
8       4     Width: 1 through 3840
12      4     Height: 1 through 2160
16      4     Number of channels: exactly 3
20      4     Encoding number: exactly 1 (RGB runs)
24      8     Exact pixel count: width multiplied by height
32      8     Encoded payload length, excluding the 64-byte header
40      4     FNV-1a32 checksum of every decoded RGB byte, in raster order
44      4     Reserved: zero
48      8     Exact whole-file length, including the header
56      8     Reserved: zero

Each payload record is four bytes: count, red, green, blue. Count is 1..255;
zero is invalid. The encoder emits maximal runs of equal RGB triples, splitting
runs longer than 255. The decoder also accepts valid shorter adjacent runs.
The sum of all counts must equal the pixel count exactly. Payload length must
be a multiple of four; trailing bytes, truncated records and extra pixels fail.
Upper words of all bounded 64-bit fields must be zero, including on a 32-bit
Zero target. Empty pictures and unsupported channel/codec values are refused.

FNV-1a32 starts at 2166136261. For each decoded byte, XOR that byte, multiply by
16777619 and retain the low 32 bits. This detects accidental damage; it does
not authenticate the sender. No existing compressed-image codec is embedded.

At the maximum dimensions, decoded RGB is 24883200 bytes. The largest encoded
file is 33177664 bytes when every adjacent pixel differs. A flat-color image
compresses substantially; noisy images can be larger than uncompressed RGB.
The file is still bounded below the native command's 32 MiB input capacity.

Shared original Zero decoder

media/zpix-decoder.zero.part contains only memory operations and Zero language
control flow; no host or file API is used. It is shared with tools/picture.zero
and media/picture-check.zero via fixed markers and our native compositor.

The caller declares the referenced Zero variables and supplies:
  @mode != 1; @error = 0
  @input = readable input pointer; @size = exact available input bytes
  @output = writable output pointer with at least 24883264 available bytes
Input and output memory ranges must be disjoint, owned and kept valid by the
caller. The decoder itself does not allocate memory. Header and record checks
reject inputs outside the format bounds before out-of-range memory accesses.

On success @error is zero; @width, @height and @pixels are valid, and decoded
RGB begins at @output+64. On failure @error is 65; the output region may contain
partial unverified bytes and MUST NOT be consumed or displayed. Only a fully
validated success may be published. Other referenced variables are scratch.

The current native command uses separate 32 MiB input and output buffers plus
4 KiB scratch (67112960 bytes total). The library does not add a desktop viewer
or an OS image command. The existing kernel is not expanded by this addition.
